The simplest assumptions to make are that the vapour behaves as a perfect gas (or that <math>p(v-w)=\text{R}\theta</math>), and that <math>\text{L}</math> is constant. This leads immediately to the simple formula
{{block center|<math>\log_e(p/p_0) = (1/\theta_0 - 1/\theta)\text{L}/\text{R},</math> . . . (22)}}
which is of the same type as <math>\log p = \text{A}+\text{B}/\theta</math>, and shows that the coefficient <math>\text{B}</math> should be equal to <math>\text{L}/\text{R}</math>. A formula of this type has been widely employed by van't Hoff and others to calculate heats of reaction and solution from observations of solubility and vice versa. It is obvious, however, that the assumption <math>\text{L} = \text{constant}</math> is not sufficiently accurate in many cases. The rate of variation of the latent heat at low pressures is equal to <math>\text{S}-s</math>, where <math>s</math> is the specific heat of the liquid. Under these conditions both <math>\text{S}</math> and <math>s</math> may be regarded as approximately constant, so that <math>\text{L}</math> is a linear function of the temperature. Substituting <math>\text{L}=\text{L}_0+(\text{S}-s)(\theta-\theta_0)</math>, and integrating between limits, we obtain the result
{{block center|<math>\log_e p = \text{A}+\text{B}/\theta+\text{C}\log_e\theta,</math> . . . (23)}}
where
{{block center|<math>\text{C}=(\text{S}-s)/\text{R},\ \text{B}=-[\text{L}_0+(s-\text{S})\theta_0]/\text{R},</math>}}

